{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787004258,"nonce":"hF7voqDZOW0ym4IVRGAlzUZAZsw=","message":"**The columns are NOT dropped, and this corrects the \"Done when\" that said they would be.**\n\nThe grievance is discharged in full: `group_key` is now `f(org, cluster, alertname, namespace-or-∅)`\n(`internal/alerts/domain/keys.go`, ADR 0038, `db/migrations/00050_derived_group_key.sql`). Neither\n`receiver` nor `source_group_key` is hashed, and the matcher is fed oto's own `group_labels`, so\n`alertmanager.yml` no longer decides how many threads oto posts. That was the whole ticket.\n\n`alert_groups.receiver` and `source_group_key` stay, as provenance. Three reasons.\n\n*Nothing reads them for routing or identity — but something does read them.*\n`GET /alert-groups?receiver=` is a shipped, documented filter\n(`internal/grouping/repository/group.go`, `internal/grouping/api/router.go`, and it participates in\nthe list ETag basis). Both are rendered in the Slack card footer, in the group detail view and in the\noutbound `webhookjson` envelope. Dropping them is a pure loss of operator-facing capability that buys\nno identity property, because the identity property is already won.\n\n*The escape hatch this ticket named does not exist.* \"`ingest_batches` already records both\" is not\ntrue in the shape it needs to be. `ingest_batches` has no `group_id`, its default retention is 14\ndays rather than 30 (`00006_ingestion.sql`), and since ADR 0038 one envelope fans out into N groups —\nso there is no way to attribute a batch's `receiver` to a particular generation. Dropping the columns\nwould destroy the provenance rather than relocate it.\n\n*Three durable artifacts already say so.* ADR 0038 (Accepted) records the columns as provenance and\nsays dropping them \"is deliberately not bundled here\"; `00050`'s comments say the same at length; SPEC\n§C.4 says \"`receiver` and `source_group_key` survive on `alert_groups` as PROVENANCE ONLY.\" Reversing\nan accepted ADR as a side effect of a ticket checkbox is the wrong instrument. If they should still go,\nthat is its own ticket with its own API-deprecation story.\n\n**What did land in this pass:** the contract drift the derived key left behind. `api/openapi/openapi.yaml`\ndescribed the group key as `(org, source, receiver, groupLabels)` in five places (the resource table,\n`listAlertGroups`, the roll-up disambiguation, `GroupKey`, `GroupDTO`); all now state the derived key,\nand `receiver`/`source_group_key` are documented as provenance. Regenerated; `generate-check`,\n`validators-check`, `dto-check` and `server-check` pass. No migration was needed, so `00053` remains free.","files":null}]}